        <div id="diy-or-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. DIY or Hire a Pro? Decide Before You Buy Anything
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first decision in crawl space mold removal is not which spray to buy. It is whether this is a job you should be doing at all. Four things settle it: how much mold there is, how easy the space is to work in, the health of the people involved, and whether the crawl space is vented or sealed. Work through them honestly before you spend money on chemicals, because the answer changes everything that follows.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size is the clearest line. EPA guidance on mold in the home says that if the moldy area is less than about 10 square feet, roughly a 3 by 3 foot patch, most people can handle the cleanup themselves. Above that, the EPA points readers to its professional remediation guidance. In a crawl space, 10 square feet is small. A few stained joist bays near a leaking drain line qualify. Fuzzy growth across most of the subfloor sheathing does not, and neither does mold that has spread into fiberglass batts between the joists, because wet insulation has to come out and be bagged, which is slow, dirty work in a confined space.
          </p>

          <p>
            Access matters more than people expect. Many crawl spaces give you 18 to 24 inches of clearance, which means lying on your back and scrubbing overhead for hours with a respirator on. If you cannot sit up, cannot reach the far corners, or the entry is a tight hatch, the job takes three times longer than you think, and the corners you cannot reach are exactly where the mold will survive. A crawl space with standing water, exposed wiring, or signs of pests is a professional job regardless of how much mold you see.
          </p>

          <p>
            Health is non-negotiable. Anyone with asthma, mold allergies, a weakened immune system or a chronic lung condition should not be doing this work, and should not be in the house while someone else disturbs the growth. Finally, consider how the space is built. A vented crawl space with a dirt or plastic floor is a fairly forgiving DIY project. An encapsulated crawl space, sealed with a heavy liner and dehumidified, that has grown mold usually means something in the system has failed, such as a torn liner, a dead dehumidifier or a plumbing leak, and the installer or a specialist is often the right first call.
          </p>

