        <div id="keep-dry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Dry It Out, Then Keep It Dry
          </h2>

          <p>
            After the fungicide has done its work, the space has to dry, and then it has to stay dry. EPA guidance is to keep indoor relative humidity below 60 percent, ideally between 30 and 50 percent. A crawl space will rarely sit at the bottom of that range, but it should not live above 60 percent for weeks at a time, and the wood moisture readings you took earlier are the real test. Put a cheap digital hygrometer in the crawl space and check it through a full summer.
          </p>

          <p>
            How you keep it dry depends on whether the crawl space is vented or sealed. Vented crawl spaces were designed on the idea that outdoor air would carry moisture away. In dry climates that can work. In humid summers it often does the opposite: warm, humid outdoor air flows in, meets the cooler ground and framing, and moisture condenses on the coldest surfaces, which is why so many vented crawl spaces are wettest in July. Closing vents without also covering the ground and drying the air simply traps moisture.
          </p>

          <p>
            An encapsulated crawl space takes the other approach: a sealed liner over the ground and up the walls, closed vents, and air that is dried mechanically or by the home's conditioned air. Building codes allow this design when the ground is covered with a continuous vapor retarder and the space is conditioned or dehumidified. Done properly, it is the most reliable long-term fix for a chronically damp crawl space, but it is a larger project than mold cleanup and is usually priced separately.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">BE HONEST ABOUT THE DEHUMIDIFIER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A basement dehumidifier is not a crawl space dehumidifier.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Consumer 50-pint units are room appliances built to sit on a basement floor and drain to a bucket or a hose. They are not designed to run unattended for months in a sealed 2-foot crawl space, and compressor units lose capacity in cool air. For a sealed crawl space, use a unit built for that duty.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            That callout matters because it is the most common mistake people make after cleanup. A standard 50-pint dehumidifier is the right tool for a damp basement, and if your crawl space opens directly into a basement that you are already drying, one unit can help both. But dropped into a sealed crawl space, a room unit runs in cool, damp air it was not designed for, with nobody checking it. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> notes that compressor units lose capacity below about 60 degrees and many stop working effectively below 50, and it covers the Aprilaire E080, which is built for continuous unattended operation in basements and crawl spaces and is the type of unit a sealed crawl space actually needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the mold you found was in the basement rather than under the house, or the crawl space air mixes freely with a damp basement, the cheaper answer is often a good 50-pint unit in the basement itself; our guide to the <a href="/best-50-pint-dehumidifier/">best 50-pint dehumidifier</a> compares those. Just be clear about which space you are drying, and check your hygrometer to prove it is working.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What a Crawl Space Mold Removal Company Charges
          </h2>

          <p>
            Published cost guides give a wide range, and they are worth reading as a sanity check rather than a quote. Angi's crawl space repair guide puts professional crawl space mold removal at roughly $1,000 to $6,000, depending on how extensive the growth is. Bob Vila, citing Angi data, gives a narrower $500 to $2,000 as the average for removing mold from a crawl space. For mold removal in general, not just crawl spaces, HomeGuide lists $1,500 to $6,000 on average, or about $15 to $30 per square foot. Those are national figures from aggregators, not our own data, and local prices vary widely.
          </p>

          <p>
            What drives the price is predictable. The area affected is the biggest factor: a few joist bays is a half-day job, while growth across the whole subfloor means days of HEPA vacuuming, scrubbing or blasting. Access is the second: a low, cramped crawl space takes longer per square foot than one you can kneel in. Insulation removal and disposal adds labor and material. Any structural repair, such as sistering a rotted joist or replacing a sill plate, is priced separately. And the moisture fix, whether a sump pump, drainage work, a vapor barrier or full encapsulation, is often a separate line that can cost more than the mold work itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you collect quotes, ask each company the same questions. What is causing the moisture, and is fixing it included? Will they remove and replace insulation? What product will they apply, and is it EPA-registered as a fungicide? Will they test or measure wood moisture afterward to show the space is dry? A company that only offers to spray and leave, with no plan for the water, is selling you the same problem again in a year. A company that insists every job needs full encapsulation, regardless of what you show them, deserves a second opinion.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a small, accessible job inside EPA's 10 square foot guidance, doing it yourself costs a respirator, gloves, goggles, coveralls, a HEPA filter for your vacuum, a bottle or two of fungicide and a moisture meter. That is a fraction of the lowest professional estimate. The gap closes quickly as the area grows, because your time in a crawl space is also a cost, and the work is miserable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the mold before fixing the water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fungicide on wet wood buys a few months at best. If gutters, grading, a leaking pipe or bare soil keep the crawl space damp, the growth returns on the same joists. Find the source, fix it, and confirm with a moisture meter that the wood is drying before you spray anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using bleach on joists and subfloor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bleach whitens the surface of wood while the water in it soaks into the grain, and Clorox's directions for its Tilex mold spray exclude wood. Mold that looks gone can still be alive below the surface. Use a fungicide registered for the job, such as RMR-141, instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying and wiping without the contact time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                RMR-141's label calls for the surface to stay wet for 10 minutes. A quick mist that dries or drips off a joist in a minute has not met the kill claim. Re-spray overhead surfaces before they dry, and use a pump sprayer on anything larger than a few square feet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving moldy insulation in place</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fiberglass batts that have been wet or grown mold cannot be cleaned. They hold moisture against the subfloor and reseed the wood you just treated. Bag them inside the crawl space, remove them, and replace them only after the moisture problem is solved and the space has stayed dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a room dehumidifier in a sealed crawl space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Consumer 50-pint dehumidifiers are built for basements and living spaces. Left unattended in a cool, sealed crawl space they lose capacity and nobody notices when they stop. A sealed crawl space needs a unit built for continuous duty there, or conditioned air from the home.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does crawl space mold removal cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Published guides give a wide range. Angi puts professional crawl space mold removal at roughly $1,000 to $6,000 depending on how much growth there is, while Bob Vila, citing Angi, gives $500 to $2,000 as an average. Insulation removal, structural repairs and the moisture fix, such as a vapor barrier or encapsulation, are often priced separately and can cost more than the mold work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does mold removal cost in general?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For mold removal anywhere in the home, HomeGuide lists an average of $1,500 to $6,000, or about $15 to $30 per square foot. Price depends on the area affected, access, what has to be removed and replaced, and whether the moisture problem is part of the job. A small patch under about 10 square feet is usually a reasonable DIY job per EPA guidance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best crawlspace mold killer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For killing mold on joists and subfloor, we would use RMR-141 RTU, an EPA-registered fungicide (Reg. No. 61178-2-93443) that the maker lists for porous and non-porous surfaces. Saturate the wood and keep it wet for 10 minutes. For treating a whole crawl space, Concrobium Mold Control applied with its fogger reaches areas you cannot scrub. Neither removes stains.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I do mold in crawl space removal myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the growth covers less than about 10 square feet, the space is reachable, nobody involved has asthma or a weakened immune system, and you have fixed the moisture source. Wear at least an N-95 respirator, gloves and goggles, HEPA vacuum and scrub the wood, apply a registered fungicide, and dry the space. Larger or wetter jobs deserve professional quotes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When should I hire a crawl space mold removal company?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hire one when growth covers more than about 10 square feet, insulation is wet or moldy, there is standing water, the space is too tight to reach every corner, or someone in the home has a health condition affected by mold. Ask every company what is causing the moisture and whether fixing it is included, or the mold will return.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does bleach kill mold in a crawl space?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bleach is the wrong product for crawl space framing. It is meant for hard, non-porous surfaces like tile, and Clorox's directions for its Tilex mold spray say not to use it on wood. On a joist it whitens the surface while water soaks into the grain. RMR's own bleach-based stain remover, RMR-86, is labelled by the maker as not killing mold at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier stop crawl space mold from coming back?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Drying the air is the best long-term prevention, with EPA guidance pointing to indoor humidity below 60 percent and ideally 30 to 50 percent. But the dehumidifier has to suit the space. A sealed crawl space needs a unit built for continuous duty there, like the Aprilaire E080. A consumer 50-pint unit belongs in a basement, not a sealed crawl space.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

